265| \begin{proposition}[Rolling without slipping down an incline]\label{prop:b1:systems-of-points:rollingincline}
266| A solid of revolution ($J = kMR^2$ about its axis) released on an
267| incline of angle $\alpha$ rolls without slipping with the acceleration
268| \[
269| a = \frac{g\sin\alpha}{1 + k} :
270| \]
271| $\tfrac57 g\sin\alpha$ for a solid sphere, $\tfrac23 g\sin\alpha$ for a disk,
272| $\tfrac12 g\sin\alpha$ for a hoop --- independent of mass and radius.
273| \end{proposition}
274|=
275| \begin{proof}
276| Energy: $Mgh = \tfrac12 Mv^2(1 + k)$ along the slope (the static friction
277| at the resting contact point does no work), so $v^2 = 2ah$ with $a$ as
278| stated; or Newton for $G$ and the moment theorem about $G$ with the
279| friction $f$: $Ma = Mg\sin\alpha - f$, $kMR^2\dot\omega = fR$, $a = R\dot\omega$.
280| \end{proof}
